WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:01.313
<v Instructor>So now let's talk</v>

2
00:00:01.313 --> 00:00:02.550
about how Lambda functions work

3
00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:04.410
when you deploy them in a VPC.

4
00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:06.243
So by default, when you deploy Lambda,

5
00:00:06.243 --> 00:00:09.060
it's deployed in the cloud of AWS.

6
00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:11.130
And so you get access to the public internet,

7
00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:13.980
you can access the public API of DynamoDB,

8
00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:16.140
but in case you have a private RDS database

9
00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:18.390
and your private VPC and private subnet,

10
00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:20.010
then it's not going to work.

11
00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:22.860
If you deploy Lambda function in a VPC,

12
00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:23.730
then the Lambda function

13
00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:26.040
is going to be assigned a security group.

14
00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:28.260
It's going to be deployed within your VPC,

15
00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:32.280
and so access to a private RDS database is going to work.

16
00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:35.190
But then once you want to access the public internet,

17
00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:37.470
you may get into some problems.

18
00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:41.220
So if the Lambda function is deployed in the private subnet,

19
00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:43.620
what you can do, is that you can deploy a NAT gateway,

20
00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:47.070
for example, or a NAT instance in a public subnet.

21
00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:48.685
And so, which is going to redirect traffic

22
00:00:48.685 --> 00:00:52.680
to an internet gateway onto the public internet.

23
00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:55.230
And the same way, if you want to access DynamoDB,

24
00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:57.840
which is a public API, first of all,

25
00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:00.240
you can access it through the NAT gateway,

26
00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:03.360
through the internet gateway to DynamoDB.

27
00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:04.350
Or you could create,

28
00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:06.900
if you wanted to keep all traffic internal,

29
00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:09.390
a VPC endpoint for DynamoDB

30
00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:10.453
and have the Lambda function

31
00:01:10.453 --> 00:01:13.230
reach out to the endpoint directly.

32
00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:15.480
Note that if you deploy a Lambda function

33
00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:18.990
in a public subnet, it will not have internet access.

34
00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:19.823
This does not work.

35
00:01:19.823 --> 00:01:21.283
The only way for it to get internet access

36
00:01:21.283 --> 00:01:23.560
is to deploy in a private subnet

37
00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:26.820
and to use a NAT device.

38
00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:31.080
Now, even if you deploy it in a private subnet

39
00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:34.410
without NAT, an internet gateway,

40
00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:37.140
the CloudWatch log feature of Lambda will work, okay?

41
00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:40.470
This is something embedded in the service itself.

42
00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:43.680
So now we know how Lambda in a VPC works,

43
00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:46.860
let's discuss how it goes for the IP.

44
00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:49.140
So when you deploy a Lambda function

45
00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:53.550
in your public cloud on AWS without a VPC setting,

46
00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:56.130
then is going to get a random public IP

47
00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:58.260
from the AWS public IP.

48
00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:00.510
And so when it accesses the internet,

49
00:02:00.510 --> 00:02:02.336
the API that you're trying to access

50
00:02:02.336 --> 00:02:05.280
just sees traffic from a random public IP.

51
00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:06.330
And this is good.

52
00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:07.800
But say you control the API

53
00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:09.075
and you want to actually filter

54
00:02:09.075 --> 00:02:11.760
and you want to have tight network security,

55
00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:14.700
and you wanna guarantee that you have a fixed IP

56
00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:17.670
that reaches it or a set of fixed IPs.

57
00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:20.586
So the way we do it is that we deploy Lambda function

58
00:02:20.586 --> 00:02:22.560
in our private subnet,

59
00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:24.630
which is going to talk to a NAT gateway,

60
00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:26.700
which is going talk to an internet gateway,

61
00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:29.460
and then to our API on the internet.

62
00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:31.320
So you may say, "Well, this is the same?"

63
00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:34.140
No, well, it's actually a little bit different,

64
00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:36.870
because when you deploy a NAT gateway

65
00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:40.260
you attach a fixed elastic IP to it.

66
00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:43.590
And so therefore, any traffic coming through the NAT gateway

67
00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:45.810
onto the internet is going to be seen

68
00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:48.180
from that fixed elastic IP.

69
00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:50.520
And so effectively, all the Lambda function traffic

70
00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:52.890
will be seen from the same elastic IP.

71
00:02:52.890 --> 00:02:54.270
And with this architecture,

72
00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:57.000
we've given our Lambda function a fixed IP

73
00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:59.430
when accessing APIs on the internet,

74
00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:02.010
and therefore we can use some kind of network security

75
00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:04.263
on the API end on the public side.

76
00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:09.300
Now, let's talk about synchronous invocations of Lambda.

77
00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:12.150
So anytime Lambda is invoked from the CLI,

78
00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:14.190
the SDK, or API gateway,

79
00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:15.810
it's going to be a synchronous invocation,

80
00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:17.550
that means that the results is returned right away

81
00:03:17.550 --> 00:03:19.740
or we wait until the results are happening,

82
00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:22.530
and any kind of error handling must happen client site,

83
00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:24.270
for example, retries,

84
00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:27.090
exponential backups, etcetera, etcetera.

85
00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:29.520
So in this example, we have the SDK

86
00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:32.070
and it's going to invoke our Lambda function.

87
00:03:32.070 --> 00:03:35.190
And then the Lambda function will do something,

88
00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:37.950
and then we'll get a response.

89
00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:40.950
And if you have a look at this architecture right here,

90
00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:43.560
the client is going to invoke the API gateway

91
00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:45.450
which is going to proxy the request to Lambda,

92
00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:47.520
which is going to return a response,

93
00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:50.790
and then the response back to the client.

94
00:03:50.790 --> 00:03:53.130
In the asynchronous invocations,

95
00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:57.720
we have SNS, we have S3, we have Amazon EventBridge,

96
00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:01.080
and Lambda will be evoked asynchronously.

97
00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:03.330
And in case there is an error on Lambda side,

98
00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:05.730
there will be retries on errors,

99
00:04:05.730 --> 00:04:07.290
so up to three total.

100
00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:08.700
And so therefore, the Lambda function

101
00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:10.080
can be run multiple times,

102
00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:11.040
and so you need to make sure

103
00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:13.277
that in case it runs multiple times,

104
00:04:13.277 --> 00:04:14.820
the processing is the same,

105
00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:16.800
and therefore it's called idempotency.

106
00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:18.930
So for example, if you take Amazon S3,

107
00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:20.430
there's a new file event

108
00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:22.980
which is an asynchronous invocation on Lambda

109
00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:24.457
and it can do retries.

110
00:04:24.457 --> 00:04:26.220
And when these retries fail,

111
00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:29.070
you can define a dead-letter queue, DLQ,

112
00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:33.690
that could be SNS or SQS for processing the failed events.

113
00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:36.660
So we've seen asynchronous and synchronous invocations.

114
00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:39.750
Now let's discuss two architectures for Lambda,

115
00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:41.880
that's for something that looks seemingly similar

116
00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:44.370
but has actually some big trade offs.

117
00:04:44.370 --> 00:04:46.860
So let's say we upload files in Amazon S3,

118
00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:51.000
and we want the S3 to trigger a notification in Amazon SNS

119
00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:54.360
and we hook a Lambda function to Amazon SNS.

120
00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:57.090
And alternatively, we do Amazon S3,

121
00:04:57.090 --> 00:04:58.500
we trigger Amazon SNS,

122
00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:01.620
but this time we send the messages into SQS,

123
00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:05.310
and SQS is going to be consumed by a Lambda function.

124
00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:06.360
So they look like the same,

125
00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:07.410
Lambda is going to be invoked

126
00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:10.710
whenever an image is put in Amazon S3.

127
00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:13.890
But which one do you think starts immediately,

128
00:05:13.890 --> 00:05:16.110
is the first one or the second one?

129
00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:18.180
Imagine you upload 1000 files a second,

130
00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:19.260
something like this.

131
00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:22.170
Well, with Amazon S3 and SNS,

132
00:05:22.170 --> 00:05:24.330
it's going to start immediately

133
00:05:24.330 --> 00:05:26.640
because, well, Amazon S3 will trigger a notification

134
00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:29.293
in Amazon SNS which will immediately invoke

135
00:05:29.293 --> 00:05:30.360
a Lambda function,

136
00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:32.670
and you can go very high on the concurrency level

137
00:05:32.670 --> 00:05:35.310
if you have a lot of notifications in SNS.

138
00:05:35.310 --> 00:05:38.010
So therefore, for every image in Amazon S3,

139
00:05:38.010 --> 00:05:40.830
Lambda is going to be triggered as fast as possible

140
00:05:40.830 --> 00:05:44.190
and you're going to get a lot of parallel executions.

141
00:05:44.190 --> 00:05:46.260
Now, if some of these do not go well,

142
00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:47.790
you may lose some data.

143
00:05:47.790 --> 00:05:49.410
You need to use a DLQ

144
00:05:49.410 --> 00:05:51.520
to make sure the failed Lambda attempts

145
00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:53.760
do end up somewhere.

146
00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:56.910
Now, if you are using the second architecture,

147
00:05:56.910 --> 00:05:59.580
then the messages go from Amazon S3 into SNS

148
00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:00.570
into an SQS queue.

149
00:06:00.570 --> 00:06:02.850
So this happens as fast as possible

150
00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:04.920
and the SQS queue will fill up.

151
00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:08.109
But then Lambda functions will obviously scale as well,

152
00:06:08.109 --> 00:06:10.440
but they will consume batch of messages

153
00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:11.970
and process them one by one.

154
00:06:11.970 --> 00:06:13.050
And so one Lambda function

155
00:06:13.050 --> 00:06:14.700
will have 10 messages, for example,

156
00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:16.260
and the second one will also have 10.

157
00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:21.260
And so the queue will not empty as fast as possible.

158
00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:23.790
So you're going to get batch execution,

159
00:06:23.790 --> 00:06:25.440
is going to be a bit more efficient,

160
00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:27.090
but there's gonna be a bit of delay

161
00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:29.520
from when the file is uploaded onto Amazon 3

162
00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:31.920
and processed by a Lambda function.

163
00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:34.020
But in case you have Lambda function failures,

164
00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:38.220
well, the messages are going to be kept in Amazon SQS.

165
00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:40.980
Okay, so that's it for the second part of Lambda.

166
00:06:40.980 --> 00:06:41.940
I hope you liked it,

167
00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:44.013
and I will see you in the next lecture.

